-- Section 1: Single Pool Inter-Token Gap --

  On one pool, a decode step cannot run while a long prefill occupies the GPU.
  The inter-token gap for in-flight requests = 1 (their own step) + any prefill
  steps that jumped ahead of them. We record the per-step gap distribution.

  Inter-token gap  P50:    6 steps
  Inter-token gap  P99:   78 steps  <- long prompts spike the tail
  [check] single pool has a heavy P99 inter-token tail

-- Section 2: Disaggregated Inter-Token Gap --

  With prefill on a separate pool, the decode pool runs only single-token steps,
  so its inter-token gap is ~1 step regardless of how long any prompt is. The
  cost moves to a one-time KV-cache transfer between pools.

  Inter-token gap  P50:    1 steps
  Inter-token gap  P99:    1 steps
  P99 jitter reduction : 78x
  [check] isolating prefill flattens the decode-latency tail

-- Section 3: KV Transfer Overhead --

  Disaggregation only pays off if moving the KV cache between pools is cheap
  relative to the decode it protects. Fast interconnect (NVLink/RDMA) is the
  enabling condition.

  Transfer 5.0 MB over NVLink ~600 GB/s  :  0.008 ms  (worth it)
  Transfer 5.0 MB over RDMA/IB ~50 GB/s  :  0.100 ms  (worth it)
  Transfer 5.0 MB over PCIe ~25 GB/s     :  0.200 ms  (worth it)
  [check] on fast interconnect, KV transfer is sub-millisecond

